//# Supervisors: long-lived tasks that outlive the code that started them,
//# restarted by policy when they stop. `Supervisor.new` makes one, and
//# only during `boot`; `Supervisor.spawn` and `Supervisor.spawn_all` start
//# work under it with a `Restart` disposition and a `Backoff` schedule,
//# and `GiveUp` says what happens when the schedule runs out.
//# `Supervisor.flush` waits for its tasks to finish. Where supervisors fit
//# in a program's life and how they shut down are language rules
//# ([Concurrency](/spec/concurrency/)).

import
    duration.Duration
    int.PositiveInt
    iter.Iter
    lists.List
    tasks.Task
    unit.Unit
end

/// Which endings make a supervisor run a task again.
///
/// `Restart` says whether a stopped task runs again. `Backoff` says when,
/// and how many times. The three values match the BEAM's `:temporary`,
/// `:transient` and `:permanent`.
///
/// A restart calls the function the spawn was given again, so whatever
/// state that function sets up is built again.
///
/// A cancelled task is never restarted, under any value. Shutdown cancels
/// tasks, and a restart then would keep the program from exiting.
pub enum Restart
    /// Never restarted. This is the default, since most work spawned under a
    /// supervisor is meant to finish.
    ///
    /// A failure is still reported on standard error.
    Temporary

    /// Restarted if it fails, which is a trap in its body. A task that
    /// returned is done.
    ///
    /// For work with an end, such as sending an email or writing an audit
    /// row: it is retried when it breaks and left alone once it finishes.
    Transient

    /// Restarted whenever it stops, whether it failed or returned.
    ///
    /// For work with no end, such as a worker draining a queue or a server
    /// reading an inbox. Such a task returning on its own is a bug: its loop
    /// took a branch it should not have, or the channel it read was closed.
    /// Under `Permanent` that is reported and the task restarted. Under the
    /// other two values the worker would stop with no report.
    Permanent
end

/// When a supervisor tries a failed task again, and how many times before
/// it gives up.
pub enum Backoff
    /// Exponential delays with jitter. The first delay is one second, each
    /// later one is double the last, and none is longer than a minute. The
    /// supervisor gives up after `max_restarts` restarts or after
    /// `max_elapsed` spent trying, whichever comes first.
    ///
    /// Both fields have defaults, so `.Exponential{}` is the schedule a
    /// supervisor has when none is named. Naming one field keeps the other's
    /// default: `.Exponential{max_restarts: 3}` still gives up after fifteen
    /// minutes.
    ///
    /// `max_restarts` counts restarts, not runs, so `max_restarts: 3` runs
    /// the body at most four times.
    ///
    /// Jitter is always applied. Without it, a supervisor restarting fifty
    /// identical tasks would retry them all at the same instant.
    ///
    /// A run longer than a minute, the longest delay, counts as recovered:
    /// the next failure starts again from the first delay with the whole
    /// count and the whole time. So a worker that fails once a month never
    /// uses up `max_restarts`.
    Exponential {
        max_restarts: Int = 10,
        max_elapsed: Duration = Duration.minutes(15),
    }
end

/// What a supervisor does when it decides a task will not run again:
/// under `Restart.Temporary` at its first failure, and under
/// `Backoff.Exponential` once `max_restarts` or `max_elapsed` runs out.
///
/// Neither choice restarts the program. `Exit` ends the process, and
/// whatever runs the process decides whether it starts again.
pub enum GiveUp
    /// The default: report the failure on standard error and carry on. The
    /// supervisor's other tasks keep running.
    Report

    /// End the process: every supervisor is drained, then the program exits
    /// with a non-zero status.
    ///
    /// Nomi does not start the program again. A service manager or an
    /// orchestrator does, and the new process runs `boot` again, so it
    /// starts with new connections, new state and new supervisors.
    ///
    /// It is a poor fit for a long-running process with nothing above it to
    /// restart it, such as a desktop application or an embedded runtime.
    /// There, use `Report` and your own monitoring.
    Exit
end

/// How long `Supervisor.flush` may block.
pub enum Wait
    /// Block until the supervisor's work finishes, however long that takes.
    Forever

    /// Block until the work finishes or this much time passes, whichever
    /// comes first. It returns as soon as the work is done. A bound past
    /// about 292 years, the longest wait the clock counts, waits that long,
    /// as `Forever` does.
    UpTo Duration
end

/// How a `Supervisor.flush` ended.
///
/// A deadline on the `Context` in force stops the work that runs past
/// it. A bound given to `flush` stops nothing and returns `TimedOut`, so
/// the caller decides what a slow flush means.
pub enum FlushOutcome
    /// Every task outstanding when `flush` was called has finished.
    Flushed

    /// The bound ran out with work still outstanding. Nothing was
    /// cancelled: the work carries on, and shutdown still bounds it.
    TimedOut
end

/// An owner for tasks that outlive the call that started them.
///
/// A `concurrent` block does not produce its value until every task in
/// it has finished, which does not suit a signup handler that responds
/// now and sends mail later. A function that spawns under a
/// `Supervisor` returns while the work keeps running, and the
/// supervisor decides how long shutdown waits for it. Every task has
/// one of these two owners.
///
/// A supervisor is a set of tasks that share a policy. Its tasks share
/// one `max_running` limit and wait on each other for a slot,
/// `Supervisor.flush` waits for all of them, and shutdown drains all of
/// them within one `shutdown_timeout`. Tasks under different supervisors
/// do not affect each other. Put work under one supervisor when it
/// should be treated the same way at shutdown and on failure, not
/// because it serves the same feature.
///
/// Supervisors are fields of the application struct and are created in
/// `boot`, one per policy the program needs:
///
/// ```nomi
/// struct App
///     context: Context
///     audit:   Supervisor
///     notify:  Supervisor
/// end
///
/// fn boot() -> Result<App, String>
///     Ok(App{
///         context: Context.root(),
///         audit:   Supervisor.new(shutdown_timeout: Duration.seconds(30), max_running: 4),
///         notify:  Supervisor.new(shutdown_timeout: Duration.milliseconds(200), max_running: 50),
///     })
/// end
/// ```
///
/// Supervised work lives in the process and is not durable: a crash
/// after the response is sent and before the email is sent loses the
/// email. That is acceptable for a welcome message and not for a
/// receipt.
pub host type Supervisor

impl Supervisor
    /// Creates a supervisor.
    ///
    /// `max_running` is how many of its tasks run at once, and it has no
    /// default. The limit protects whatever the tasks call, such as a
    /// connection pool or a rate-limited service, and only the program
    /// knows that number. Work reaches a supervisor from call sites all
    /// over the program, so no limit would mean no limit for the whole
    /// run. A supervisor that holds one long-lived worker has
    /// `max_running: 1`.
    ///
    /// `shutdown_timeout` is how long shutdown lets this supervisor's work
    /// run before cancelling it. Work that finishes within it is never
    /// cancelled. With a zero or negative timeout, shutdown cancels the
    /// work at once. Supervisors drain at the same time, each within its
    /// own timeout, so a 30-second one and a 200-millisecond one do not
    /// wait for each other. When the timeout runs out, the runtime cancels
    /// what is left, waits up to one second for it to unwind and run its
    /// deferred calls, and then abandons it, so a task that never reaches
    /// a safe point cannot hang shutdown. Boot's deferred calls run after
    /// the drain, so they close what the work used only once the work has
    /// stopped. A timeout past about 292 years, the longest wait the clock
    /// counts, lets the work run until it finishes.
    ///
    /// `restart`, `backoff` and `on_give_up` say what happens when a task
    /// stops: see `Restart`, `Backoff` and `GiveUp`.
    ///
    /// A supervisor can be created only during `boot`: in `boot` itself or
    /// in a constructor `boot` calls, which is how a server type owns its
    /// supervisor and its restart policy. A call to `Supervisor.new` that
    /// `boot` does not reach is a compile error. Every supervisor lives as
    /// long as the program and has its own `max_running`, so one created
    /// per request would give each request its own limit: `max_running: 8`
    /// under a thousand requests at once would be eight thousand tasks.
    pub fn new(
        max_running:      PositiveInt,
        shutdown_timeout: Duration = Duration.seconds(5),
        restart:          Restart = .Temporary,
        backoff:          Backoff = .Exponential{},
        on_give_up:       GiveUp = .Report,
    ) -> Supervisor
        Supervisor.new_exact(max_running, shutdown_timeout, restart, backoff, on_give_up)
    end

    // The five-argument extern, with no defaults of its own.
    //
    // The split exists so that the four defaults above have exactly ONE owner,
    // and it is `NaiveDateTime.new` over `new_exact`'s shape rather than a new
    // mechanism. A `host fn` binds no defaults of its own: a Go function that
    // padded a short argument list itself would make the declaration
    // documentation of a Go line rather than the authority for it, and leave
    // the two free to drift. A Nomi body's defaults are read by the IR builder
    // from this declaration, so there is one encoding and nothing to drift
    // from.
    //
    // The split is also the reason `Supervisor.new` can be lowered at all:
    // internal/irbuild refuses a `host fn` with a trailing default outright, by
    // name, and says so: the declared
    // default of an extern is documentation of whatever the implementation does
    // with a short argument list, and a builder filling from it would agree with
    // a second encoding by luck.
    host fn new_exact(
        max_running:      PositiveInt,
        shutdown_timeout: Duration,
        restart:          Restart,
        backoff:          Backoff,
        on_give_up:       GiveUp,
    ) -> Supervisor

    /// Starts `body` as a task under `supervisor` and returns at once.
    ///
    /// The call never blocks. When `max_running` tasks are already
    /// running, the new task waits for a slot on its own.
    ///
    /// It returns a `Task`, as `Task.spawn` does, and this one need not be
    /// awaited, because the supervisor drains it at shutdown. Use the
    /// handle to cancel the task or to read its `Task.outcome`, or discard
    /// it with `_ =`.
    ///
    /// The body must return `Unit`. Nobody has to await supervised work,
    /// so a returned value would have nowhere to go, and a returned `Err`
    /// would be dropped without a report. Wrap the call in a function that
    /// handles the failure and reports it.
    pub host fn spawn(supervisor: Supervisor, body: () -> Unit) -> Task<Unit>

    /// Starts one task per item of `source` under `supervisor`, each
    /// running `f` on its item.
    ///
    /// It takes no `max_running`: the supervisor's limit applies to these
    /// tasks and to every other task spawned under it.
    ///
    /// `f` returns `Unit`, as all supervised work does, so there are no
    /// values to collect. Use the handles to cancel tasks, or pass them to
    /// `Task.await_all` to wait for this batch and not for everything
    /// under the supervisor. It consumes the whole source, so a source that
    /// is `Iter.is_infinite` traps before any task is spawned.
    pub host fn spawn_all<T>(
        supervisor: Supervisor,
        source:     Iter<T>,
        f:          (T) -> Unit,
    ) -> List<Task<Unit>>

    /// Blocks until every task outstanding under `supervisor` has finished.
    ///
    /// It cancels nothing. Shutdown cancels what is left when
    /// `shutdown_timeout` runs out; `flush` waits and reports. Call it
    /// where queued work must have gone out before the program reports
    /// success, or in a test, so the background work a call started
    /// finishes before the test asserts on it.
    ///
    /// A task is outstanding if it was spawned before the call: `spawn`
    /// adds its task before it returns, so work started on an earlier line
    /// is always included, and work a flushed task spawns is waited for
    /// too. Work spawned after the call is not waited for.
    ///
    /// With no `bound` it waits as long as the work takes. Pass
    /// `Wait.UpTo(d)` to limit the wait; when the time runs out it returns
    /// `FlushOutcome.TimedOut` and the work carries on:
    ///
    /// ```nomi
    /// case Supervisor.flush(App.notify, Wait.UpTo(Duration.seconds(5)))
    ///     FlushOutcome.Flushed  -> report_sent()
    ///     FlushOutcome.TimedOut -> carry_on()
    /// end
    /// ```
    pub fn flush(supervisor: Supervisor, bound = Wait.Forever) -> FlushOutcome
        Supervisor.flush_bounded(supervisor, bound)
    end

    // The two-argument extern, with no default of its own. See `new_exact`
    // above: one owner for `bound: Wait = Wait.Forever`, and the only shape the
    // IR builder lowers.
    host fn flush_bounded(supervisor: Supervisor, bound: Wait) -> FlushOutcome
end
